                    <div class="bg-green-50 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mb-3">Pros</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.pros.map((pro) => (
                          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-green-800">
                            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-green-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg>
                            {pro}
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-3">Cons</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-red-800">
                            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>
                            {con}
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-gray-100">
                    <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-1">The Bottom Line</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                  </div>

                  <a
                    href={amazonLink(product.amazonUrl, product.name, product.id)}
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="sponsored nofollow noopener"
                    class="mt-6 block bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white text-center py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm"
                  >
                    Check Price &rarr;
                  </a>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="real-upgrade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Makes a Real Upgrade, Not Just a Pricier Gadget
          </h2>

          <p>
            A real upgrade does the job he already does, noticeably better, every time. The Fellow Stagg EKG is not better because it costs more; it holds a set temperature and pours slowly enough for proper coffee, and he notices both every morning. That is why our <a href="/best-electric-kettle/">electric kettle guide</a> ranks it first. If you cannot name the improvement in one sentence, such as holds an edge longer or charges over USB-C, he will not feel it either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Platform matters more than people expect. A Milwaukee M18 impact driver is a superb gift for a man with red batteries and a useless one for a man on yellow DeWalt packs, because the best tools are sold bare. Our <a href="/best-impact-driver/">impact driver guide</a> and <a href="/best-cordless-drills/">cordless drill guide</a> break the platforms down. Likewise, a man who owns a click wrench gets a genuine step up from the angle-reading digital wrench in our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">torque wrench guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is longevity, where most unique Christmas gifts for men go wrong: they are built for the unwrapping, not the next ten years. Forged knives, enamelled cast iron, rotomolded coolers and lifetime-guaranteed socks all point the other way. The wow is quieter, but it does not fade by February.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="consumables" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Consumables He Would Never Buy Himself
          </h2>

          <p>
            Consumables solve the clutter problem completely. They get used up and leave nothing behind but the memory of the good version, and the top of most consumable categories is still not very expensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Socks are the classic. Most men buy cheap multipacks and wear them until the heels go translucent. A five-pack of Darn Tough merino hikers costs more than he would ever spend on socks, which is exactly why it works, and the lifetime guarantee means the gift keeps replacing itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coffee is the other easy win. A few bags of whole beans from a good local roaster beat most coffee gadgets, and if you are buying the Fellow grinder or kettle, add a bag so he can use them on Christmas morning. Other reliable choices: a bottle of what he already drinks, premium blades for the saw he uses most, or the replacement filters he keeps meaning to buy. The rule holds: a new cologne is a gamble, a bottle of the one he wears is a sure thing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="experiences" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Experience Gifts for Men Who Have Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Experience gifts for men are the cleanest answer to the man who truly has everything, because an experience cannot duplicate anything he owns. Choose for who he is: a track day thrills a man who loves driving and stresses one who does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skill-based experiences suit men who already own every tool. Think a one-day class in knife sharpening, welding, blacksmithing, woodturning, butchery or barbecue; community colleges, maker spaces and specialist shops often run weekend workshops. Access experiences work too: tickets to the game he watches on television, a guided fishing day, a round at the course he has always wanted to play.
          </p>

          <p>
            Book something specific with a date rather than a vague voucher, which is just one more thing for him to organize. And remember time itself: a night away that is booked and planned can beat any object on this page. If you want something to unwrap, pair it with a related gift, such as the YETI for the fishing trip. Check cancellation terms, because winter weather moves outdoor bookings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="annoyances" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fix an Annoyance He Has Stopped Noticing
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most appreciated gifts often come from paying attention rather than spending more. He resets the clocks after every outage, sweeps up dog hair every evening, works in a coat in the garage all winter. None of it feels worth fixing to him, which is why fixing it feels like a gift.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outages are the classic. A power station like the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 runs a fridge, router and lamp through a typical outage with no fuel or fumes; our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station guide</a> covers the field, and <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">our sizing guide</a> does the capacity math. For floors, the Roborock Saros 10R from our <a href="/best-robot-vacuum/">robot vacuum guide</a> turns the nightly sweep into something that happens while he is out. For a man hauling bottled water home, the under-sink RO system from our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">water filtration guide</a> ends the habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The garage gets the biggest reaction. A hardwired heater from our <a href="/best-garage-heater/">garage heater guide</a> gives him back three or four months a year, but the DR-975 needs a dedicated 240V circuit, so budget for an electrician and book early, because December is busy for trades.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="personalized" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Personalized Christmas Gifts: What Is Worth Personalizing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Personalized Christmas gifts for men have a mixed reputation for good reason. A name etched on a cheap cutting board is personal and forgettable at once. Engraving makes an object almost impossible to return or regift, so only personalize something good enough to keep for decades.
          </p>

          <p>
            A premium multi-tool is the best candidate, which is why the Leatherman ARC is here: Leatherman lists it as customizable on its own site, and a tool he carries daily becomes one he hands down. A quality knife or watch works the same way, and YETI's site lets you build a custom colorway for the Tundra 45, personalization without a name in sight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep engraving short: initials, a date, or a few meaningful words, and nothing tied to a job or team he might leave. Personalized items usually cannot be returned and take extra production time, so for Christmas 2026 aim to order by the first week of December and check each retailer's posted cut-off. If you are unsure he will love the object itself, skip the engraving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="luxury" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Luxury Christmas Gifts for Men That Justify the Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            Luxury Christmas gifts for men are easy to find and hard to get right. Our test: would the expensive version still be the right choice at the ordinary price? If yes, the premium buys something real. If the only argument is the logo, it is a receipt, not an upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three picks clear that bar. The Le Creuset Dutch oven is the one piece of expensive cookware our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/">induction cookware guide</a> calls genuinely worth it, because it lasts long enough to be inherited. The Roborock Saros 10R, our top robot vacuum, buys reliability. The Segway Navimow X3, our top robotic mower, buys back every summer Saturday.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Navimow has the most conditions: it suits open lawns, needs a clear-sky spot for its antenna, and comes in sizes matched to lawn area, so know his yard before ordering; our <a href="/best-robotic-lawn-mower/">robotic lawn mower guide</a> has the details. It will also sit boxed until spring. At this level, one large gift beats several medium ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-not-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What Not to Buy the Man Who Has Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Single-trick gadgets are the biggest trap. Whiskey stones, novelty bottle openers and most things sold as unusual Christmas gifts for men follow the same arc: a laugh on Christmas morning, a drawer by February.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not buy a cheaper version of something he owns in a better version, such as a budget multi-tool for a man who carries a Leatherman. It tells him you did not notice what he has. Be cautious with clothing and fragrance unless it is a direct replacement of something he already loves. Skip open-ended subscriptions, which become another bill to cancel, and avoid cordless tools on the wrong battery platform or gadgets that need an app he refuses to install.
          </p>

          <p>
            If he turns out easier to buy for than you thought, our <a href="/best-christmas-gifts-for-men/">main Christmas gift guide for men</a> covers the broader field, and our <a href="/christmas-gifts-for-men-under-50/">Christmas gifts for men under $50</a> guide handles stocking stuffers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing novelty over use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The commonest mistake is picking something because it is surprising rather than because he will use it. Surprise lasts a morning; use lasts years. For a man who owns everything, a better version of a daily object will outperform a clever gadget every time, even if it looks less exciting under the tree.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring his battery platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cordless tools run only on their own brand's batteries, and the best ones are often sold bare. Buying a Milwaukee tool for a DeWalt owner means he needs a new battery and charger or never uses it. Check the color of the batteries on his garage shelf before buying anything cordless.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Personalizing something mediocre</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Engraving locks a gift to him permanently, so it cannot be returned, resold or quietly regifted. Only personalize something good enough to keep for decades, such as a premium multi-tool or a quality knife. A name on a cheap item turns a forgettable gift into one he cannot get rid of.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting installation and setup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Several of the best annoyance-fixing gifts need work after unwrapping. A hardwired garage heater needs an electrician and a dedicated circuit, an under-sink RO system needs plumbing, and a robot mower needs a clear-sky antenna spot. Budget the time and cost, or the gift becomes a chore.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving big orders too late</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Personalized items, custom colorways and direct-ship products often take longer than ordinary orders, and December shipping is slower while popular models sell out. Order engraved and big-ticket gifts by early December, and check each retailer's posted holiday cut-off rather than assuming standard delivery times.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

