        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do you get a man who has everything for Christmas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Get him a better version of something he uses every day, a premium consumable he would never buy himself, an experience, or a fix for a recurring annoyance he has stopped noticing. Our top pick is the Fellow Stagg EKG kettle, because a daily upgrade gets used every single morning. Skip novelty gadgets, which usually end up in a drawer by spring.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are some unique Christmas gifts for men?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most unique Christmas gifts for men are usually well-chosen rather than strange: a forged chef's knife to replace the dull one he uses nightly, a MagnaCut-bladed multi-tool he will carry for decades, or a hands-on class in something he has always wanted to try. Uniqueness comes from how well the gift fits him, not from novelty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are experience gifts better than physical gifts for men?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a man who truly has everything, often yes, because an experience cannot duplicate anything he already owns. Book something specific with a date, such as a class, a guided fishing day or tickets to a game, rather than a vague voucher. Pairing the experience with a related physical gift gives him something to unwrap too.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I spend on a gift for a man who has everything?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no right number, and spending more does not rescue a poorly chosen gift. A well-chosen consumable around one hundred dollars often beats an expensive gadget. Our picks run from a five-pack of Darn Tough socks to a robotic mower costing thousands. For tighter budgets, our under-$50 guide has stocking stuffers and smaller gifts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What personalized Christmas gifts for men are worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Personalize only what is good enough to keep for decades: a premium multi-tool, a quality knife, a watch or a durable cooler. Keep engraving short, such as initials or a date. Avoid personalizing cheap novelty items, because engraving makes them almost impossible to return or regift. Order early, since personalized items take longer to make.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are luxury gifts worth it for a man who already has everything?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only when the premium buys something real, such as durability, reliability or time saved. A Le Creuset Dutch oven that lasts a lifetime or a robot vacuum that rarely needs rescuing clears that bar easily. A luxury item whose only advantage is its logo usually does not. One excellent large gift beats several medium ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I not buy a man who has everything?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Avoid single-trick gadgets, cheaper versions of things he already owns in a better version, clothing or fragrance unless you know his exact taste, open-ended subscriptions, and cordless tools on a battery platform he does not use. Each of those either adds clutter or creates a chore, which is the opposite of what a good gift should do.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

